(656821, 66691, 18856, '<td valign="top">congrats for the last place ;-) Now I get it: it''s a naked lady leaning on some kindof mirror or shiny wall maybe. Did you use a camera-phone on this ? I appreciate the DavidHamilton-ian look of it. And also the atmosphere.... Remembers me somehow of an 80''s song "Big in Japan" (Alphaville). In the end "the beauty is in the eye of the beholder"... ;-)</td>', 'congrats for the last place ;-) Now I get it: it''s a naked lady leaning on some kindof mirror or shiny wall maybe. Did you use a camera-phone on this ? I appreciate the DavidHamilton-ian look of it. And also the atmosphere.... Remembers me somehow of an 80''s song "Big in Japan" (Alphaville). In the end "the beauty is in the eye of the beholder"... ;-)', '2004-04-07 07:59:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(656825, 67440, 6999, '<td valign="top">Of course there is sadness in the death of a child, that almost goes without saying. <br/> <br/>Your photo, however, is really very blunt. It is entirely a portrait of a tombstone - what sense of location there is is simply entirely of a graveyard, exactly as one would expect, and makes no contrast, comment or difference to the view of the stone. As a study of the texture of the stone, the light is not useful - there is little sense of smoothness, or roughness, and the framing and depth of field does not allow for a perception of contrast with the grass, trees, and what surroundings are visible. This is what I mean by ''blunt'' ... there is nothing else going on here, in photographic terms. <br/> <br/>Had it been my own project, I think I would have tried to show something of the atmosphere of the cemetary/graveyard, or perhaps tried to contrast the heaviness, solidity and smoothness of the stone with the surrounding grass, leaves, or whatever. Those choices would depend on the nature of the location. Colour might have been effective in that task - it seems to be a black stone, and to contrast that with a vibrant green in the grass, or perhaps the leaves of the tree (shooting upwards from the ground), might have made colour a good choice for the shot - black and white is not necessarily the only way to ''do'' sombre moods. <br/> <br/>Just some thoughts. <br/> <br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'Of course there is sadness in the death of a child, that almost goes without saying.  Your photo, however, is really very blunt. It is entirely a portrait of a tombstone - what sense of location there is is simply entirely of a graveyard, exactly as one would expect, and makes no contrast, comment or difference to the view of the stone. As a study of the texture of the stone, the light is not useful - there is little sense of smoothness, or roughness, and the framing and depth of field does not allow for a perception of contrast with the grass, trees, and what surroundings are visible. This is what I mean by ''blunt'' ... there is nothing else going on here, in photographic terms.  Had it been my own project, I think I would have tried to show something of the atmosphere of the cemetary/graveyard, or perhaps tried to contrast the heaviness, solidity and smoothness of the stone with the surrounding grass, leaves, or whatever. Those choices would depend on the nature of the location. Colour might have been effective in that task - it seems to be a black stone, and to contrast that with a vibrant green in the grass, or perhaps the leaves of the tree (shooting upwards from the ground), might have made colour a good choice for the shot - black and white is not necessarily the only way to ''do'' sombre moods.  Just some thoughts.  Ed', '2004-04-07 08:00:56.000000', NULL, 0),
